//
// Probe: `encode.base64`, `decode.base64` and `pull.workbench`, with their
// workbench forms.
//
// **base64 carries a whole value, not its text.** `encode.base64` serialises
// its operand through the wire format and base64s *that*, so `42
// encode.base64 decode.base64` gives the integer back and not the string
// `"42"`. The reference's own codec and the reference's own wire bytes (D20).
//
// **Only the round trip is here, never a bare `encode.base64`.** The wire
// bytes embed the value's identity, so the base64 *text* differs between the
// two engines (D88: a decoded value mints a fresh identity) — and F14's
// normalisation cannot reach inside a base64 string to hide it. It is
// `debug.dump`'s `_` arm again: the bytes are the subject, so no normaliser
// applies. A round trip is reproducible because the decode restores the value
// and the dump is normalised as usual.
//
// **`pull.workbench` names values off the workbench into a dict**, and the
// names come from the stack while the values always come from the workbench —
// so `pull.workbench.` takes its name list off the workbench and then drains
// what is left of it. Member order is F15's, which the capture normalises.
//
"probe: a base64 round trip returns the value, not its text" println
42 encode.base64 decode.base64
"hello" encode.base64 decode.base64
[ 1 2.5 "x" ] encode.base64 decode.base64
true encode.base64 decode.base64

"probe: the workbench forms round-trip on the workbench" println
7 { true } ?. encode.base64. decode.base64.

"probe: pull.workbench names workbench values into a dict" println
10 { true } ?.
20 { true } ?.
[ "a" "b" ] pull.workbench
